# ---- RSA-PSS (CertificateVerify, and X.509): a security deviation, any salt length ----------
# RFC 8446 4.2.3: with RSASSA-PSS "the length of the Salt MUST be equal to the length of the
# output of the digest algorithm". mbedTLS 3.6 checked it (ssl_tls13_generic.c:310 at 72aa6873,
# expected_salt_len = PSA_HASH_LENGTH). mbedTLS 4.1.1 does not: ssl_tls13_generic.c:344 verifies
# with MBEDTLS_PK_SIGALG_RSA_PSS, which is PSA_ALG_RSA_PSS_ANY_SALT
# (tf-psa-crypto/include/mbedtls/pk.h:52), and vectors.c does the same. Wycheproof's "s_len
# changed" vectors are correct signatures with another salt length: accepted. The salt length is
# not what makes PSS unforgeable (RFC 8017 9.1 allows any salt length, empty included), so this is a conformance
# deviation, not a forgery; every other modified signature is refused, which the run checks. It is
# a security-deviation (rsa-pss-any-salt), so it shows on the SECURITY DEVIATIONS line and
# CRYPTO_STRICT=1 fails on it. X.509 certificate signatures get the same treatment: 4.1.1 parses
# RSASSA-PSS's saltLength and drops it (x509.c:748-753) and verifies with the same any-salt
# algorithm (x509_crt.c:2145); x509/pss-salt.json's lines are in the bundle section below.
