# Security-relevant fail-open deviations are marked "fail-open:<tag>" at the head of their
# reason: they print on the FAIL-OPEN DEVIATIONS line of every run, and are counted, with their
# lines, as insecure outcomes, not as passed: intermediate-eku, noncritical-nameconstraints,
# cn-fallback, any-eku, public-suffix-wildcard. The same behaviour in listed order (the
# x509-misordered section) is marked too and shows as <id>@given.

# A cross-signed pair in the order listed, the ICA that does not verify first: mbedTLS takes the
# first same-named untrusted issuer and does not try the other (it skips a candidate that does
# not verify only when it is trusted: x509_crt.c:2298). OpenSSL 3.6.2 refuses it the same way.
x509 tc=signatures::cross-signed-one-good reject fail-closed, as listed: the first same-named untrusted issuer is used whether its signature verifies or not (x509_crt.c:2298)

# ---- X.509 chains out of order (x509-misordered: limbo's own order) ------------------------
# mbedTLS looks for each certificate's parent only among the certificates after it in the
# Certificate message (x509_crt.c:2378, child->next) and does not go back. So a chain whose
# intermediates are not in path order can be refused although a path exists, and a chain
# with alternative issuers can be judged on whichever comes first. In server order (x509-limbo)
# these cases get the outcome recorded there.
limbo.json/given tc=pathlen::intermediate-pathlen-may-increase reject fail-closed, out of order: a parent that comes before its child in the list is never found (x509_crt.c:2378); in server order it is accepted
limbo.json/given tc=pathlen::max-chain-depth-1-self-issued reject fail-closed, out of order: a parent that comes before its child in the list is never found (x509_crt.c:2378); in server order it is accepted
