# ---- Security deviations: ESP-IDF's certificate bundle (x509-bundle) ------------------------
# security-deviation <tag>: accepted where it should be refused, by design of the code under
# test, not a bug of this version. Printed on the SECURITY DEVIATIONS line of every run; the run
# fails when one no longer shows, and CRYPTO_STRICT=1 counts them as failures.
# tls_engine.c's TLS_OPEN_CA_BUNDLE mode verifies with esp_crt_bundle_attach() (tls_engine.c:274).
# ESP-IDF 6's esp_crt_bundle.c (at ESP_IDF_COMMIT) installs esp_crt_verify_callback() and, with
# CONFIG_MBEDTLS_CERTIFICATE_BUNDLE_CROSS_SIGNED_VERIFY (on by default in v6.0.3, and in
# sdkconfig.v6.0.3), a CA callback (esp_crt_bundle.c:601): for each certificate, the bundle
# entry named by its issuer comes back as a trusted CA made of that name and key alone
# (esp_crt_bundle.c:498-577, ca_istrue at :522, no validity). mbedTLS then checks the chain
# against it as against any trusted CA, the certificate profile included: a SHA-1 signature
# below it (BAD_MD) and an RSA-1024 bundle key (BAD_KEY) are refused, which 3.6's callback let
# through (bundle-sha1-below-root, bundle-root-key-size-unchecked: tests/crypto run with
# sdkconfig.v6.0.3-nocross shows both again). The made-up CA has no validity, so mbedTLS marks
# it EXPIRED, and the verify callback clears that for it (esp_crt_bundle.c:331-340): an
# expired root still verifies what it signed. Each chain is also run with the root as the CA
# chain (tc=...::ca), where it is refused.
# The verify callback's own signature check picks the algorithm from the bundle key's type
# alone: RSA is always PKCS#1 v1.5 (esp_crt_bundle.c:196-199), and 3.6's check that the key can
# do the certificate's signature algorithm (pk_can_do, esp_crt_bundle.c:52 at c8fc5f64) is gone.
# So a certificate that names ECDSA or RSASSA-PSS but carries a PKCS#1 v1.5 signature by a
# bundled RSA root is accepted (mbedTLS's own check refuses it; the callback then clears the
# flags). Algorithm confusion, not a forgery: the root's key must have signed those bytes.
x509 tc=bundle-rsa::alg-says-ecdsa::bundle security-deviation bundle-sig-alg-unchecked a leaf naming ecdsa-with-SHA256 with a PKCS#1 v1.5 signature by the RSA bundle root is accepted (esp_crt_bundle.c:196-199)
x509 tc=bundle-rsa::alg-says-pss::bundle security-deviation bundle-sig-alg-unchecked a leaf naming RSASSA-PSS with a PKCS#1 v1.5 signature by the RSA bundle root is accepted (esp_crt_bundle.c:196-199)
x509 tc=pss-salt::salt-not-as-declared::ca security-deviation rsa-pss-any-salt a certificate whose RSASSA-PSS salt differs from its declared saltLength is accepted (x509.c:748-753, x509_crt.c:2145)
x509 tc=pss-salt::salt-not-as-declared::bundle security-deviation rsa-pss-any-salt the same under a bundle root: mbedTLS checks it against the CA made from the entry (x509_crt.c:2145)
# The bundle is looked up by the raw bytes of a certificate's issuer name (esp_crt_bundle.c:295),
# where RFC 5280 7.1 compares names case-insensitively and across string types, as mbedTLS does
# in CA mode: a valid chain naming its root in another case or string type is refused.
x509 tc=bundle-idf6::T2-issuer-case-differs::bundle reject fail-closed: the issuer is looked up by its raw bytes (esp_crt_bundle.c:295), so ROOT A does not find Root A
x509 tc=bundle-idf6::T2b-issuer-printable::bundle reject fail-closed: the issuer is looked up by its raw bytes (esp_crt_bundle.c:295), so a PrintableString Root A does not find the UTF8String one
x509 tc=bundle-sha1-confused::U4-confused-sha1::bundle security-deviation bundle-sig-alg-unchecked the same with SHA-1: BAD_MD is masked off (esp_crt_bundle.c:348) and the callback verifies the PKCS#1 v1.5 SHA-1 signature (esp_crt_bundle.c:172, :196-199)
x509 tc=bundle::expired-root::bundle security-deviation bundle-root-validity-unchecked the bundle keeps no validity, and the verify callback clears EXPIRED on the CA it makes from a bundle entry (esp_crt_bundle.c:331-340): an expired root still verifies what it signed
# ---- Known vulnerabilities of the pinned mbedTLS -------------------------------------------
# known-vulnerable <CVE> <first fixed version>: the vector must still show the vulnerable outcome.
# The run stays usable as a regression gate, prints the CVE on a line of its own before the
# final one, and fails once mbedTLS behaves correctly, so the line is removed with the bump.
# CRYPTO_STRICT=1 counts these vectors as failures, so the run is red on them.
# None for mbedTLS 4.1.1: CVE-2026-49300 (3.6.0's line) no longer shows; 4.1.1's ChangeLog lists
# its fix (ChangeLog:72-77), and x509/cve-2026-49300.json's cases now get their own verdict.
